        },
        "AIAH_Day_Action": "My body feels rested (sleep 10) and mostly healthy (health 9), though a slight feeling of illness persists (illness 3). Hunger and thirst are very low (1), which is good. My energy isn't full (5), but it's usable. I have this large fish I just caught. The sun is warm, and the air is calm. My diary reminds me that resting when energy is low or illness is present is important, but this fish feels like an immediate need. It feels... 'fresh' now, but I sense that might not last. The description calls it 'fresh' and mentions 'spoilage' as a concern. I don't know what spoilage is, but it sounds like the fish will become unusable if I wait too long. I need to do something with it. How do I eat it? It has scales and insides that look different from the main part. My past experience with berries involved just putting them in my mouth. This fish seems different, larger, and more complex. Maybe I need to get the inside parts out? The description mentions 'processing' the fish. How? I have my hands. There are rocks everywhere. Maybe a rock with a sharp edge could help? I remember seeing rocks near the stream. I will look around this large flat rock I'm on, and nearby on the bank, for a smaller rock that has a sharper edge than others. Once I find one, I will use the flat rock I am sitting on as a base. I will place the fish on the flat rock and try to use the sharp-edged rock to cut the fish open. I'll try to scrape away the scales and remove the soft parts inside, separating the fleshy meat that looks like the main part of the fish. This seems like it will take effort, and my energy is only 5, so I must be careful. I should drink some water from the stream first, as my 'Hydration Management' skill reminds me this is good practice even when thirst is low. While I work on the fish, I need to remain aware of my surroundings, like my 'Environmental Observation' skill suggests, especially since the fishing might have attracted unseen attention, even though the danger level is low. My immediate goal is to make the fish ready to eat before this 'spoilage' happens.",
