    "state_metadata": {
        "AIAH_prompt_from_ES": "The sun is warmer now. The flat rock where you prepared the fish smells strongly and buzzes with flies. Your portion of raw fish rests nearby, but it won't stay fresh for long in this heat. The stream flows gently, and the woods offer shade and potential resources further inland. What do you want to do next?",
        "Day_Environment_Pre_Action": {
            "terrain": "Mixed woodland beside a slow stream. A large, flat rock near the bank is stained and smells strongly of fish waste, attracting flies. A sharp-edged rock lies nearby. Signs of previous foraging (depleted plants) are visible along the immediate bank.",
            "temperature": 27,
            "weather": "Clear skies, warm sunshine, gentle breeze carrying the distinct scent of fish decay downstream.",
            "food_availability": "One portion of prepared raw fish (gutted/scaled, starting to show early signs of spoilage due to warmth). Fish waste (guts, scales) on the flat rock attracting more insects. Nearby edible plants remain depleted. Root vegetables identifiable further inland away from the stream.",
            "water_availability": "Abundant fresh water from the stream.",
            "shelter_options": "Good tree canopy overhead provides shade. A dense thicket nearby offers concealment. The flat rock is functional but contaminated and smelly.",
            "wildlife": "Increased number of flies buzzing around the fish waste. Birds and squirrels remain cautious and distant, possibly alerted by the scent or activity. Stream fish remain in deeper water. The strong scent continues to risk attracting scavengers or predators.",
            "danger_level": "Low-Medium (No immediate predators visible, but the persistent strong scent of fish waste significantly increases the probability of attracting unwanted attention from scavengers/predators over time. Risk of raw fish spoilage is increasing.)"
        },
        "AIAH_Day_Action": "My body feels rested (sleep score 10), yet my energy is very low (3) and I still feel unwell (illness 4). My hunger and thirst are low (2 and 1). The most striking things here are the strong smell of fish waste coming from the flat rock I remember using before, and the flies buzzing around it. The rock is stained and looks unclean now. There's prepared fish nearby, but it looks different, maybe less fresh than before, and the description mentions 'spoilage'. My diary reminds me that resting is crucial when energy is low and I'm unwell (Conscience Note 1 & 2), and that untreated things can be risky (Conscience Note 2). Eating the spoiling fish seems like a bad idea, especially when I'm already ill. The smell is also concerning; the environment update notes it increases the risk of attracting unwanted attention. My energy is too low (3) to try foraging for the root vegetables further inland, which sounds like hard work, and the plants near the stream are depleted anyway (Memory Day 3, Environment Update). The flat rock is no longer a good place to rest due to the contamination and smell. Therefore, my priority must be to conserve my limited energy while minimizing risk. I will first drink some fresh water from the stream, being careful to take it from a spot away from the waste on the rock if possible. Then, I will move a short distance away from the smelly, contaminated flat rock and the spoiling fish, perhaps finding a spot under the shade of the trees closer to the dense thicket I remember seeing (Memory - thicket_01), but not so far that it uses too much energy. Resting there will help conserve energy, which my diary indicates is very important right now. From this new resting spot, I will observe my surroundings carefully, watching for any animals attracted by the fish smell and keeping an eye on the area where the root vegetables were mentioned.",
        "AIAH_Day_Action_Possibility": "possible",
        "Reality_Check_Agent_Reasoning": "The human's energy level is very low (3) and they are unwell (illness 4), which limits strenuous activity. However, the planned actions consist of drinking water, moving a short distance to a nearby shaded area for rest, and observing the surroundings. These are low-exertion activities aimed at conserving energy, which is appropriate given the health status. The environment is warm (27C) with clear skies and abundant water, posing no immediate environmental hardship for these minimal actions. Although the low energy makes significant travel or foraging difficult, the described actions (drinking, short move, rest, observation) are well within the human's current physical capabilities.",
