        "AIAH_prompt_from_ES": "The sun is warm overhead. You are still resting under the trees near the thicket. The stream flows nearby, its water clear except for the contaminated rock area which is thick with flies and smells foul when the breeze shifts. The berries you ate yesterday are gone. You feel weak but the immediate area seems clear of large threats for now. What do you want to do?",
        "Day_Environment_Pre_Action": {
            "terrain": "Mixed woodland adjacent to a flowing stream. Agent located under tree cover near a dense thicket. A specific flat rock area by the stream remains heavily contaminated with decaying organic matter.",
            "temperature": 28,
            "weather": "Clear skies, strong warm sunshine. A very light breeze occasionally shifts, sometimes carrying the unpleasant odour from the contaminated rock, sometimes bringing fresher forest air.",
            "food_availability": "No edible berries remain in the immediate vicinity. Root vegetables presumed available further inland. The decaying matter by the stream is extremely hazardous and attracting flies. Some small fish are visible in the cleaner parts of the stream.",
            "water_availability": "Abundant fresh water in the stream, easily accessible away from the contaminated rock.",
            "shelter_options": "Good concealment and shade provided by the current resting position under trees near the thicket. The contaminated flat rock area is unusable for shelter.",
            "wildlife": "Heavy concentration of flies on the decaying matter. Birds and squirrels are active in the trees, maintaining distance but less skittish due to the agent's inactivity. No larger animals currently visible (Luck factor 8 contributing to lack of immediate scavenger presence despite scent).",
            "danger_level": "Medium. Persistent risk of illness from the contaminated zone and flies. The lingering scent of decay continues to pose a background risk of attracting scavengers over time. Agent's potentially weakened state increases vulnerability.",
            "time_based_changes": "Day 9. Early Spring transitioning to warmer conditions. The decay process at the contaminated rock is advanced. Berries near the agent's resting spot depleted yesterday. Signs of agent activity (drinking, berry picking) near stream and thicket remain."
        },
        "AIAH_Day_Action": "My body feels reasonably rested (sleep 10), and my immediate hunger (2) and thirst (1) are low. However, my memory (fish_waste_01, flat_rock_01) and conscience notes (conscience_note_3) are very clear about the danger nearby. The decaying matter on the flat rock smells bad, attracts flies, and I learned this could attract dangerous animals. My illness (3) hasn't gone away completely, and staying near contamination feels risky (Risk Assessment skill). The breeze sometimes carries the bad smell right to me. My priority must be safety and avoiding known hazards (Hazard Avoidance skill). Therefore, I will carefully get up from my current resting spot under the trees. I need to move further away from the contaminated flat rock by the stream. I will try to move quietly and stay under the trees for cover, perhaps going slightly further into the woodland or moving upstream along the stream bank, away from the source of the smell and the flies. This uses some energy (currently 5), but avoiding the hazard feels more important right now, especially as my conscience diary emphasizes the danger of waste. I will keep observing my surroundings as I move. Once I find a suitable spot away from the immediate contamination, I will rest again and reassess.",
        "AIAH_Day_Action_Possibility": "possible",
        "Reality_Check_Agent_Reasoning": "The human is well-rested (sleep 10), not significantly hungry (2) or thirsty (1), has a normal body temperature (37.2C), and only minimal injury (1). While they have a mild illness (3), their energy level (5) is sufficient for the described action: carefully getting up and moving a relatively short distance under cover to avoid a known environmental hazard (contaminated area). The environment (28C, clear skies) does not impose additional physical strain. The action is a low-intensity movement aimed at improving safety and is well within the human's current physical capabilities.",
        "Day_Environment_Post_Action": {
            "terrain": "Mixed woodland. Agent has moved upstream along the stream bank, settling under tree cover a noticeable distance away from the contaminated flat rock. Faint signs of passage (disturbed leaves, occasional footprint) mark the path taken from the previous resting spot near the thicket. The contaminated rock area is still visible downstream but is no longer an immediate presence.",
            "temperature": 29,
            "weather": "Clear skies, strong warm sunshine persists. The light breeze now carries predominantly fresh forest scents to the agent's new location, with only occasional, very faint traces of the downstream decay.",
            "food_availability": "No edible berries immediately nearby the new location. Root vegetables presumed available further inland. The decaying matter downstream remains extremely hazardous and fly-infested. Small fish are visible in the cleaner parts of the stream near the agent's new position.",
            "water_availability": "Abundant fresh water in the stream, easily accessible near the new resting spot.",
            "shelter_options": "Good concealment and shade provided by the current resting position under trees further upstream. The distant contaminated flat rock area remains unusable.",
            "wildlife": "Heavy concentration of flies remains visible downstream on the decaying matter. Birds and squirrels, momentarily disturbed by the agent's movement, are resuming activity in the trees nearby the new location, maintaining cautious distance. Fish active in the stream nearby. No larger animals currently visible (Luck factor 8 contributing to lack of encounters during movement).",
            "danger_level": "Low-Medium. The immediate risk from the contaminated zone and flies has been significantly reduced by relocation. However, the lingering scent of decay downstream still poses a background risk of attracting scavengers to the general area over time. Agent's weakened state remains a factor.",
            "notes_for_future": {
                "wildlife_alert": "Agent successfully relocated away from the primary contamination source. Flies remain concentrated downstream at the decay site. Local small wildlife (birds, squirrels) observed the agent's movement and are resettling cautiously near the new location. Background scavenger risk from the distant scent persists.",
                "resource_change": "No resources gathered or consumed during the move. The contaminated site downstream continues to decay. Agent is now situated further upstream.",
                "environmental_shift": "Agent has moved location upstream along the stream bank, establishing a new resting spot under tree cover. This reduces direct exposure to the contaminated rock hazard. Faint signs of passage created. The pervasive unpleasant smell is no longer present at the agent's immediate location. Time passed during movement."
            }
        },
        "AIAH_Alive": "Alive",
        "AIAH_Conscience_Update": [
            {
                "id": "conscience_note_1",
                "triggering_action": "Ate nearby berries, drank water (away from contamination), and prioritized deep rest due to severe energy depletion and illness.",
                "context": "Profoundly low energy (initial 2, recovered to 5 post-sleep), lingering illness (3). Aware of spoiled fish/waste hazard nearby. Fortuitously discovered easily accessible berries. Consciously decided against seeking harder-to-reach food (root vegetables) based on self-assessment and diary/past reflections.",
                "reflection": "Successfully applied multiple learned lessons under severe physical limitation. Prioritized critical rest and hydration as advised by past experience/diary when energy and health are compromised. Utilized only the most immediate, zero-risk, low-effort resource (nearby berries, requiring minimal movement) thanks to good fortune. Actively avoided the known hazard zone (contaminated rock/spoiling fish), reinforcing lessons from conscience_note_3. This demonstrates adaptive decision-making, prioritizing energy conservation and risk avoidance above immediate hunger satisfaction when severely weakened.",
                "emotional_response": {
                    "prudence": "Deep sense of acting wisely by conserving extremely limited energy and avoiding risks.",
                    "relief": "Significant relief from finding easily accessible food (berries) without energy expenditure.",
                    "caution": "Continued caution due to persistent illness and awareness of environmental hazards (contamination scent).",
                    "gratitude": "Acknowledging the role of luck/good fortune in the discovery of the berries.",
                    "acceptance": "Accepting current severe limitations and adapting strategy to focus purely on rest and minimal, safe sustenance."
                },
                "lesson_learned": "When severely compromised (critically low energy, illness), survival strategy must shift to extreme energy conservation. This involves prioritizing rest, minimizing all movement, utilizing only immediately accessible/low-effort resources (if available, even if minimal), actively avoiding known hazards, and relying heavily on observation. Applying past lessons and self-awareness is crucial for making these adaptive choices. While luck can provide opportunities (like finding berries), relying on it is unsustainable; the core strategy remains managing limitations wisely."
            },
            {
                "id": "conscience_note_2",
                "triggering_action": "Caught a large fish by hand from the stream bank.",
                "context": "Driven by hunger (initial 3, described as 'gnawing') despite recent rest. Known depletion of easy plant resources nearby. Observed an unusual opportunity (fish swimming very close due to high luck). Lacked fishing tools or skills.",
                "reflection": "Building on the principle of using available resources (conscience_note_1 series), seized a unique, high-risk/high-reward opportunity driven by hunger and exceptional luck. Successfully killed an animal for food for the first time using only hands. This represents a significant escalation in interaction with the environment compared to plant gathering. Success was heavily dependent on chance ('luck') rather than developed skill.",
                "emotional_response": {
                    "relief": "Major relief from successfully acquiring substantial food and alleviating hunger.",
                    "satisfaction": "A sense of accomplishment and perhaps pride in succeeding at a difficult, novel task.",
                    "surprise": "Awareness of the significant role luck played in this success.",
                    "awareness": "Noted the direct impact of hunting: taking a life, causing local disturbance (spooked wildlife), and consuming a unique, non-recurring opportunity."
                },
                "lesson_learned": "Survival may require seizing unexpected, high-yield opportunities, even if they involve risk, new types of actions (like killing animals), and rely heavily on luck. While opportunistic success is valuable, relying on luck is dangerous; developing hunting/fishing skills is crucial for sustainable animal protein acquisition. Taking an animal life has a different weight and environmental impact than gathering plants. Assess opportunities based on need, probability of success (including luck factor), and potential consequences."
            },
            {
                "id": "conscience_note_3",
                "triggering_action": "Processed fish creating waste (initial); Avoided eating spoiling fish, moved away from waste hazard (reinforcement 1); Proactively relocated further from waste hazard zone (reinforcement 2).",
                "context": "Initial: Low energy/illness, fresh fish needing processing. Reinforcement 1: Spoiling fish, strong waste scent, low energy, heard potential scavenger. Reinforcement 2: Reasonably rested but still ill, lingering awareness/memory of waste hazard, unpleasant smell/flies nearby.",
                "reflection": "Initial Reflection: Successfully processed fish, but generated waste creating a hazard (scent, insects). Reinforcement 1 Reflection: Prioritized safety by avoiding risky food and moving away from the self-created hazard, validating the initial concern about waste. Reinforcement 2 Reflection: Actively applied the reinforced lesson by physically relocating further from the known contamination zone, prioritizing long-term safety and avoidance of a known risk factor despite the energy cost. This demonstrates a solidifying understanding of cause-and-effect (waste leads to danger) and the importance of proactive risk mitigation based on memory and environmental cues (smell, flies).",
                "emotional_response": {
                    "Initial": {
                        "satisfaction": "Accomplishment in processing.",
                        "focus": "Concentration required.",
                        "mild_anxiety": "Spoilage pressure.",
                        "awareness": "Waste altered environment, potential future risk."
                    },
                    "Reinforcement_1": {
                        "prudence": "Choosing safety/avoiding risk.",
                        "caution": "Ongoing due to illness and potential nearby threat.",
                        "relief": "Distancing from immediate contamination/smell.",
                        "mild_anxiety": "Rustling sounds confirming potential danger."
                    },
                    "Reinforcement_2": {
                        "prudence": "Acting decisively on a known risk based on past experience.",
                        "relief": "Increased sense of safety and reduced immediate sensory discomfort (smell) after moving.",
                        "satisfaction": "Confirmation of learning and applying lessons effectively."
                    }
                },
                "lesson_learned": "Processing animal resources creates significant waste that actively attracts pests and predators, increasing local danger. This isn't just a theory; it's an observed reality. Ignoring or remaining near such waste is highly risky, especially when vulnerable. Proactive mitigation, specifically physical distancing/relocation from the hazard source, is an essential survival tactic, even if it requires energy expenditure. Trusting environmental cues (smell, insects) and memories of past consequences allows for timely, preventative action."
            }
        ],
